  "plot_summary": "Strange returns home, exhausted from his \"unending battle against the supernatural forces which menace mankind!\"&nbsp; He finally succumbs to sleep, but when he opens his eyes, he sees \"a mysterious figure cloaked in darkness...covered with an aura of evil!\"&nbsp; He finds his powers useless!&nbsp; (My guess is that he hasn't actually woken up - and sure enough, only a couple of panels later, I find I am correct!)&nbsp; \"I am caught in the Nightmare World!\" (<i>Horrors</i>!)\n\nTurns out he \"forgot to utter the protective chant which keeps me safe from harm when I shut my eyes in repose!\"&nbsp; His enemy Nightmare has been waiting for a mistake like this!&nbsp; And Strange is finally captured in a sphere and deposited before Nightmare himself (his 1<sup>st</sup> full appearance as it turns out).&nbsp; He proceeds to give Strange a preview of all the horrible things he can do to him, because apparently, \"only <i>you</i> have defeated me in the past!\"\n\nStrange is bound in irons, but apparently Nightmare's focus on Strange has allowed \"the Gulgol\" to appear, Nightmare's \"mortal enemy from the netherworld!\"&nbsp; Nightmare can never defeat him because he never sleeps!&nbsp; Nightmare has no choice but to free Strange and ask for his help.&nbsp; Strange is easily able to turn the Gulgol away, leaving Nightmare incredulous.\n\nBut it turns out that Dr. Strange only had <i>hypnotized</i> Nightmare into thinking the Gulgol was there - he used his brain to escape!&nbsp; To which Nightmare can only cry \"Curse you!!&nbsp; You outsmarted me!!\"&nbsp; and Strange then makes his escape with Nightmare left only to call out \"I'll get you yet, accursed one!&nbsp; I can wait for all <i>eternity</i> if need be!\"\n\nStrange then awakens and is safe once more.&nbsp; As he stares out at the dawn over the city, the editorial blurb reads:\n\n<i>Hark to these words, loyal reader - one day, many years hence, you will proudly relate these <b>Dr. Strange</b> tales to a new generation - a generation which will envy the fact that <b>you</b> were privileged to have read the earliest of these trend-setting tales!</i>",
  "grade_one_liner": "Write something",
  "subtitle": "Write something",
  "review_text": "Words...fail...me....&nbsp; Without a doubt one of the <i>worst</i> Doctor Strange stories of all time!&nbsp; It is the poster child of \"let's make this up as we go along\" -- which unfortunately the subject matter lends itself heavily to getting away with.&nbsp; I was very tempted to give this a FR 1.0 rating, but I figured that at least the follow-up to the very brief Nightmare appearance from the debut issue #110 was worth a half point.&nbsp; That and the notion that a character such as Nightmare would sensibly have an enemy who did not need sleep as his presumably-unbeatable foe.&nbsp; Ultimately, these two things rescued the story from getting bottom marks.&nbsp; Nightmare indicates that only Strange has ever beaten him, but we're still told nothing about that encounter.\n\nIt was so simple to work out what had happened in the opening panels, that of Strange having fallen asleep.&nbsp; Nightmare looked just plain silly and hardly menacing.&nbsp; Strange's use of hypnosis to fool him into freeing him felt like a total cop-out.&nbsp; And Nightmare's dialog, particularly at the end, was right out of the late late show\" - in other words, absolutely barf-worthy.\n\nAnd to top it all off, the editorial blurb about being able to tell your grandchildren that you were \"privileged\" to have read such dreck came across as exactly the opposite -- as a warning!\n\nTo any shepherd interested in herding a new generation toward Marvel, this tale is a danger I would <i>shield</i> them from!\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nThe Dr. Strange half of <i>Strange Tales</i> is fast descending into the story I dread most reading every month.&nbsp; The character itself has the potential to show promise, but these stories do nothing to showcase that promise, and quite frankly, they threaten to put me to sleep every time I sit down with them!&nbsp; <i>Brutal</i>!",
  "highlight": "Write something"
}